INT. PARKED SEDAN, PARKING GARAGE LEVEL P3 - NIGHT

Concrete pillars. Flickering lights. One car, engine off, windows fogged.

MARISOL CASTELLANOS-OKOYE (30s, black coat, gloved hands on the wheel) sits upright, eyes on the rearview. On the seat beside her, a phone, face-up, on speaker.

HANDLER (V.O.)
(over phone, flat)
Status.

MARISOL
Parked. Third level.

HANDLER (V.O.)
He's coming down the stairwell. Two minutes.

MARISOL
Armed?

HANDLER (V.O.)
Assume yes.

She nods. Puts a hand under the seat, finds a pistol, brings it up. Checks it. Sets it in her lap.

HANDLER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Marisol.

MARISOL
Yeah.

HANDLER (V.O.)
You don't have to do this.

MARISOL
I know.

HANDLER (V.O.)
Walk away. Tonight. I'll cover it.

MARISOL
No.

She stares at the mirror. A shadow moves at the far end of the garage. She stiffens.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
(whispering)
I see him.

HANDLER (V.O.)
Hold.

MARISOL
He's looking at the car.

HANDLER (V.O.)
Hold.

The shadow stops. Takes a step. Another. A man emerges from behind a pillar, hands in his pockets, face lost in shadow. He walks toward the sedan with a deliberate, easy stride.

MARISOL
Oh, no.

HANDLER (V.O.)
What?

MARISOL
It's my brother.

Silence on the line. The man reaches the driver's window and taps once on the glass. She doesn't move. He taps again. She lowers the pistol, slides it back beneath the seat, and rolls the window down an inch.

Hands shaking, she picks up the phone, and without a word, ends the call.
